% ============================================================================
% earth-layer-thickness — how thick each of Earth's internal layers is, where
% the source states a number (adj-facts-stdlib, GEOLOGY).
% ============================================================================
% A sibling to the already-shipped `earth-layers.adj` (`has_state(layer,
% state)`, ONE physical-state fact per layer): a new
% `earth_layer_thickness(layer, thickness_km)` table names the THICKNESS in
% kilometers the SAME already-cited USGS page states for a layer, decoded
% from spans already sitting unused inside `earth-layers.adj`'s own header
% and provenance block — no new WebFetch. That table's `state` column has
% room for only ONE fact per layer (rigid/semi_solid/liquid/solid); the
% thickness figures the very same USGS sentences also carry were left
% un-tabled:
%
%   mantle → 2900
%     "Below the crust is the mantle, a dense, hot layer of semi-solid rock
%      approximately 2,900 km thick."
%   outer_core → 2200
%   inner_core → 1250
%     "Unlike the yolk of an egg, however, the Earth's core is actually made
%      up of two distinct parts: a 2,200 km-thick liquid outer core and a
%      1,250 km-thick solid inner core."
%
% Recall is a binding query:
%
%     ? earth_layer_thickness(mantle, $ThicknessKm)      % 2900
%
% This is a native `table` (ADJ-TABLES RS-5, a TWO-column table like
% `earth-layers.adj`'s own `has_state`): each `row` lowers to a ground
% relation `earth_layer_thickness(layer, thickness_km)` carrying the
% citation, so the lookup above is the ordinary SLD binding query — the
% engine returns the thickness WITH its source, on the CPU, with zero model
% calls, and abstains on any layer the cited spans do not give a number for
% (`crust`, whose own span in `earth-layers.adj` says only "very thin
% compared with the other two," with no figure to decode).
%
% Only THREE of `earth-layers.adj`'s four layers have a thickness figure in
% the ALREADY-cited spans; this table is deliberately narrow rather than
% inventing a number the source does not state. The query also runs
% BACKWARD — bind a thickness and recall which layer it names:
%
%     ? earth_layer_thickness($Layer, 1250)   % inner_core
%
% Provenance: reproduces, byte-for-byte, the SAME two spans already quoted
% inside `earth-layers.adj`'s own provenance block, from the SAME USGS "This
% Dynamic Earth" page that table already cites — no new WebFetch, only a
% different schema decoding a part of each already-verified quote. An ADJ
% `table` carries ONE provenance envelope (per `earth-layers.adj`'s own
% convention), so `source`/`locator`/`trust` below hold the core span
% (which fixes BOTH outer_core and inner_core at once); the mantle row's own
% span is listed above for independent auditability. `trust authoritative`
% — the same tier `earth-layers.adj` already carries (USGS, a primary U.S.
% government source).
% (See ../README.md; feedback_nothing_human_authored,
% feedback_adj_only_shipped_artifacts.)
% ============================================================================

table earth_layer_thickness {
    columns layer, thickness_km

    row (mantle, 2900)          % "approximately 2,900 km thick"
    row (outer_core, 2200)      % "a 2,200 km-thick liquid outer core"
    row (inner_core, 1250)      % "a 1,250 km-thick solid inner core"

    source "Unlike the yolk of an egg, however, the Earth's core is actually made up of two distinct parts: a 2,200 km-thick liquid outer core and a 1,250 km-thick solid inner core."
    locator "https://pubs.usgs.gov/gip/dynamic/inside.html"
    trust authoritative
}
